  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1130 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nThursday night through Tuesday:\n\n500 mb cluster analysis shows an anomalous ridge axis over the \narea Thursday night should push to the east Friday into \nSaturday. This should continue to support south southwest winds \nat the surface during this time, gradually increasing and \nbecoming gusty Friday into Saturday. This will allow for \ncontinued warm air advection, and bring high temperatures into \nthe lower to middle 60s Thursday into Saturday. \n\nHighs may get a little warmer each day if low level mixing is \nmore robust and more sunshine can occur. Record highs for \nMilwaukee and Madison are generally in the middle 60s to lower \n70s these three days. These highs are supported by ensemble \nmembers and very small spreads in ensemble high temperatures.\n\nModels and ensemble means then generally show a cold front \nmoving through the area mainly later Saturday evening into \nSunday morning. There looks to be a fairly high probability of \nrain (60 to 80 percent) with the frontal passage, as ensemble \nmembers are all showing measurable precipitation with rainfall \namounts of perhaps one quarter to one half inch, highest in the \nwestern parts of the area. It does not look like much if any \nthunder will occur, as instability is very low. \n\nGusty west northwest winds behind the front should bring strong\ncold air advection and much colder temperatures by early next \nweek. Highs may only reach the upper 30s Monday and Tuesday. \nThis trend is also supported by the ensemble members. High \npressure moving into the region should bring dry conditions.\n\nWood",
